title: Definitions
---
body:

Euclid didn't a single word for rectangle, but two words meaning right-angled parallelogram.

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="500" height="220" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img src="defII.gif" hspace="10" alt="II.Def.1"></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "Def.II",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;50,40",
            "B;point;free;140,40",
            "C';point;perpendicular;B,A;0;0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;B,C',140,180",
            "D;point;parallelogram;A,B,C",
            "ABCD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;black;random",
            "E;point;free;270,40",
            "F;point;free;450,40",
            "G;point;free;400,180",
            "H;point;parallelogram;E,F,G",
            "EFGH;polygon;quadrilateral;E,F,G,H;0;0;black;random",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G",
            "L;point;lineSlider;EG,300,110",
            "K';point;parallelogram;E,H,L;0;0",
            "K;point;intersection;E,F,K',L",
            "M;point;parallelogram;E,K,L",
            "EKLM;polygon;quadrilateral;E,K,L,M;0;0;black;random",
            "N;point;intersection;K,L,H,G",
            "LN;line;connect;L,N",
            "O;point;intersection;M,L,F,G",
            "LO;line;connect;L,O",
            "Q';point;midpoint;L,G;0;0",
            "Q;point;midpoint;L,Q';black;gray",
            "P;point;cutoff;L,K,L,Q;black;gray",
            "R;point;cutoff;L,M,L,Q;black;gray",
            "PQR;sector;arc;R,Q,P;0;0;gray;0"
        ],
    });
</script>
</center>

According to the first definition, the rectangle *ABCD* illustrated on the left is contained by the lines *AB* and *BC,* and this rectangle can be called the rectangle *AB* by *BC.* Of course, it could also be called the rectangle *BC* by *CD,* or two other names.

On the right, in the parallelogram *EFGH,* there is a diameter *EG* with a parallelogram *LNGO* about it and the two complements *KLOF* and *MHNL,* and these three parallelograms together make up the gnomon. In other words a gnomon is an L-shaped figure made by removing a parallelogram from a larger similar parallelogram.

The word *gnomon* comes from the Greek word meaning carpenter's square. A carpenter's square is an L-shaped tool with its two sides at right angles. Euclid's use of the word is looser as he doesn't require that the sides be at right angles.

Euclid illustrated gnomons by arcs of circles around the inner vertex. In this example, the gnomon is called the gnomon *PQR.*
